---
description: Security non-negotiables. Always applies, including during spikes.
alwaysApply: true
---

These hold even in throwaway code, because they are the ones that are expensive
or impossible to fix after the fact. If I ask you to break one, say so out loud
before you do.

## Secrets

- Server secrets live only in server-side environment variables.
- Any variable prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, `VITE_`, `PUBLIC_`, or `EXPO_PUBLIC_` is
  shipped to the browser and is effectively published. Never put a service-role
  key, API secret, signing key, or database URL behind one of those prefixes.
- No secrets in code, comments, fixtures, seeds, test files, or commit messages.
- When you need a new secret, add it to `.env.example` with a placeholder value
  and tell me, rather than inventing a value.

## Authorization

- Authorization is part of the query, not a check after the fetch. Filter by the
  owner or tenant key in the `where` clause. Fetching first and checking second is
  a bug even when it currently returns the right answer.
- Every route handler declares its access level explicitly. If a handler is
  reachable without a session, there must be a comment saying so deliberately.
- Never trust an identifier from the client to imply ownership. A request that
  says `userId` is a claim, not a fact. Derive identity from the session.

## Input

- Parse every external input with a schema at the boundary, then derive the type
  from the schema. Do not declare an interface and cast.
- That includes request bodies, query and path params, webhooks, third-party API
  responses, uploaded files, and environment variables at boot.
- Reject unknown fields rather than passing them through to a database write.

## Queries

- Parameterized queries only. Never build SQL by string concatenation or template
  interpolation, even for internal values.
- Every list query has a limit with a default and a hard maximum.
- Never expose a raw database error, stack trace, or internal id to a client.
  Generic message out, detail to the logs.

## Output

- No `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, or equivalent with user-supplied
  content unless it has passed through a sanitizer, and say which one.
- Serialize explicitly. Pick the fields to return rather than spreading a database
  row into a response, because the next column someone adds will leak.

## Outbound requests

- The server never fetches a URL supplied by a user without an allowlist of hosts.
- Set a timeout on every outbound request. A hung upstream should not hold a
  connection open indefinitely.

## Migrations

- Destructive changes (drop, rename, type narrowing, non-null backfill) go in
  their own commit, never bundled with a feature.
- Expand first, migrate, then contract in a later deploy.
